.TH G-UNITY7-CLEANUP 8 "November 2026" "g-unity7-desktop" "G Unity7"
.SH NAME
g-unity7-cleanup \- remove the Ubuntu Unity packages that G Unity7 replaces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B g-unity7-cleanup
.RB [ \-n | \-\-dry\-run ]
.RB [ \-y | \-\-yes ]
.RB [ \-k | \-\-keep
.IR package ]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B g-unity7-cleanup
removes duplicate Ubuntu Unity applications and other installed alternatives
of the
.B g-unity7-desktop
applications, like Gentoo's
.BR "emerge \-\-depclean" .
.PP
The Ubuntu Unity installer installs the
.B ubuntu-unity-desktop
task, and apt marks its packages as manually installed, so
.B apt autoremove
never removes them. apt also keeps every installed alternative of a relation
such as
.BR "vlc | showtime" .
.PP
For each
.B g-unity7-desktop
relation that has several installed alternatives, such as
.BR "atril | papers | evince" ,
the script asks which package to keep. It also asks when a different
alternative is installed instead of the first one, because apt does not
install
.B eog
from
.B "eog | eom | loupe"
when
.B eom
is installed. You can keep the first alternative (recommended) or another one,
which is installed if needed. When several alternatives are installed, you can
also leave them all unchanged. A kept first alternative is marked as
automatically installed, and any other kept alternative stays manually
installed. The other installed alternatives are removed, including those you
installed yourself, unless other packages need them.
.PP
On a system installed from the Ubuntu Unity image, the script then marks
.BR ubuntu-unity-desktop ,
the packages it depends on or recommends that
.B g-unity7-desktop
does not use, and the task packages that these packages and the removed
alternatives depend on (such as
.B file-roller
for
.BR nemo-fileroller )
as automatically installed, and removes them with autoremove. Packages kept by
a choice are not marked. Only packages that autoremove actually removes are
marked. Packages that are still needed, for example by
.B ubuntu-desktop
on Ubuntu, which shares most of the task, are left as they are.
Packages that
.B ubuntu-unity-desktop
depends on or recommends and that apt keeps only as an installed alternative
of another relation, such as
.B lightdm-gtk-greeter
for
.BR lightdm-greeter ,
are removed directly if no other package is removed with them.
The script recognizes such a system by
.B ubuntu-unity-desktop
or
.BR ubuntu-unity-settings ,
which only the image installs. Installing
.B g-unity7-desktop
already removes
.BR ubuntu-unity-desktop ,
which depends on
.BR pulseaudio .
The first run that makes changes writes
.I /var/lib/g-unity7/ubuntu-unity
for later runs, because it removes
.B ubuntu-unity-settings
as a duplicate. On other systems, these packages are left as they are.
.PP
The Ubuntu Firefox and Thunderbird packages install snaps. The G Unity⁷ PPA
provides them as regular packages with Unity's global menu, built from the
Debian sources. Their version is lower, so
.B g-unity7-apt-pin
keeps the Ubuntu packages until the script installs the G Unity⁷ packages. If
a snap is installed, the script asks whether to install the G Unity⁷ package
instead, together with its language packages for the languages in use
.RB ( LANG
and
.B LANGUAGE
in
.IR /etc/default/locale ,
and the language that each user chose).
It then copies the snap profiles of every user with a UID from 1000 to 59999
and of the owner of each directory in
.I /home
(which finds the users of a network directory too), if the home directory is
accessible, to the profile directory of the package
.RI ( ~/.mozilla/firefox ,
.IR ~/.thunderbird ,
or
.I ~/.config/mozilla/firefox
and
.I ~/.config/thunderbird
if only they exist, as Firefox and Thunderbird create them when
.I ~/.mozilla
or
.I ~/.thunderbird
does not exist;
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is taken to be
.IR ~/.config ),
as that user, so that a link in a home directory cannot make it change
anything else. Each copy goes to a temporary directory first and is moved into
place when it is complete, so that an error, such as a full disk, leaves no
part of a profile behind. The preview shows the size of each copy. A copy that
would leave less than 100 MB free on its file system is not offered; free some
space and run the script again. If root cannot read a home directory, for
example a network one, the preview says so, and its snap profiles are not
copied. The snap profiles in an encrypted home directory that is not mounted
are not found either. These profiles stay in
.I ~/snap
when the snap is removed, and the script does not offer them again; copy them
by hand.
The snap profiles are taken from where Firefox and Thunderbird look for them:
.I ~/snap/firefox/common/.mozilla/firefox
if
.I ~/snap/firefox/common/.mozilla
exists, and
.I ~/snap/thunderbird/common/.thunderbird
if it exists, or else from the directory of the installed snap revision,
.I ~/snap/firefox/<revision>/.config/mozilla/firefox
and
.IR ~/snap/thunderbird/<revision>/.config/thunderbird ,
where Thunderbird 160 creates new profiles.
If the user already has profiles in the package directory, for example from
the Ubuntu package used before the snap, the snap profiles are added to them
with
.B (snap)
after their names, and no profile is replaced. A profile group of Firefox
(the Profiles menu) is copied whole: its other profiles, which only the
database of the group in
.I Profile Groups
lists, keep their names, so the copy fails if one of them is taken. Other
profile directories that profiles.ini does not list are copied too. A
profile with an absolute path is not copied; the preview names those in the
snap directory, which are removed with the snap. The snap's default profile
becomes the default profile of the package, unless the package was installed
before; then its default profile stays, and the snap profiles can be opened
from
.BR about:profiles .
If the snap profile directory is a link to the package directory, which the
Thunderbird snap makes when
.I ~/.thunderbird
has profiles from the Ubuntu package used before the snap, nothing is copied,
and the snap's default profile becomes the default profile of the package in
the same way.
If the package directory already exists without profiles.ini and has a file
or directory of the same name as the snap profile directory, such as a
profile left behind, the snap profiles are added in the same way.
Finally, the script removes the snap. snapd saves a snapshot of the snap
data, but when the removal fails, it drops the snapshot after it has already
deleted the data; the copied profiles stay. Then remove the snap with
.BR "sudo snap remove" ,
or run the script again. When a copy fails, the snap stays installed, and the
copies of the other users are made. The next run continues the switch: it does
not copy the profiles that were copied, unless the snap changed them since
then, and the snap's default profile still becomes the default profile of the
package that the first run installed. While the snap is
running, the script offers nothing for it. Close the application and run the
script again. If the snap is started while the script runs, the script
leaves it installed and says what to do. A G Unity⁷ package older than
the snap is not offered, because Firefox and Thunderbird do not open a profile
that a newer version used. Run the script again after the package is updated.
A G Unity⁷ package that apt cannot install is not offered either, and the
other changes are made. This happens when GTK 3 is installed for another
architecture too, such as i386 for Steam or Wine, and the PPA does not have
its G Unity⁷ version for that architecture.
A Firefox or Thunderbird package from another source, such as Mozilla's APT
repository, stays as it is, because it may be newer than the G Unity⁷
package.
If the snap is not installed, the script also offers the G Unity⁷ package
with its language packages, and recommends it. It mentions Evolution if it is
installed instead of Thunderbird; the choice between them is part of this
offer, not of the alternatives above. Evolution is not removed.
.PP
The script also removes the desktop files of snaps that are not installed from
.IR /var/lib/snapd/desktop/applications .
The Ubuntu image leaves those of the snaps that the installer does not
install, such as Thunderbird, and the Dash shows them with a missing icon.
It also removes the mount units in
.I /etc/systemd/system
of snap revisions that are not installed and whose
.I .snap
file does not exist, such as old revisions of the base snaps. They fail at
every boot.
.PP
Installing
.B g-unity7-desktop
replaces PulseAudio with PipeWire, and apt removes
.B pulseaudio
without its configuration files. Its autostart file then starts a program
that no longer exists. The script purges these configuration files.
.PP
On a system from the Ubuntu Unity image, the script also removes the
packages that the installer leaves. The installer copies the live system,
which has Fcitx 4 with
Japanese and Chinese input, and does not remove it.
.B im-config
no longer supports Fcitx 4, so it cannot be selected as an input method, but
the Dash shows its launchers. G Unity⁷ uses IBus. Fcitx 4 also pulls in the KDE
Frameworks and the KDE
.BR systemsettings .
The image also has Kvantum, the Qt style of the installer, GParted, an
advanced partition editor
.RB ( gnome-disk-utility
does the basic tasks), and the libraries and the snap seeding tool of the
installer, which it does not remove with Calamares. All packages of the image
are manually installed. The
script marks these packages as automatically installed, and autoremove
removes them unless other packages need them. The script removes
.B kdialog
of the image directly, because apt keeps it as an alternative of
.B zenity
and it keeps the KDE Frameworks 6, unless other packages would be removed
with it.
.PP
The script shows all changes and asks for confirmation. It stops without
changes if apt would then make other changes than shown, for example after
unattended-upgrades updated the package lists in the meantime. To keep a
package, give it with
.BR \-\-keep .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show the changes without making them. The script still asks about the
alternatives, so that the list shows the result of your answers. Root is not
needed, but without it, the snap profiles of other users are not shown.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask. Keep or install the recommended alternative of each relation,
install the G Unity⁷ Firefox and Thunderbird packages instead of the snaps,
with the copies of the snap profiles, remove the snaps, and make the changes
without confirmation.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep " \fIpackage\fR"
Neither mark nor remove the package. An automatically installed package is
marked as manually installed, so that autoremove keeps it.
.B \-\-keep firefox
or
.B \-\-keep thunderbird
leaves Firefox or Thunderbird as it is. The option can be given more than
once.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/g-unity7/ubuntu-unity
The system comes from the Ubuntu Unity image. Purging
.B g-unity7-desktop
removes it.
.TP
.I /var/lib/g-unity7/switch-firefox\fR, \fP/var/lib/g-unity7/switch-thunderbird
A switch from the snap to the package that has not finished, until the snap
is removed.
.TP
.I .g-unity7-snap-copy
In the profile directory of the package: the copy of the snap profiles of the
switch is done.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR apt (8),
.BR apt-mark (8)
